# Tier D — the FULL SAT Competition 2024 + 2025 main tracks (plan §8.1: "SC 2024
# + SC 2025 full 400-instance sets, 5000 s, memory capped at 16 GB to mirror
# competition conditions").  Frozen 2026-08-03.  This is the gate set for
# non-negotiable N3 and for gates T4 (exceed Kissat-public PAR-2 on the SC25
# set) and T5 (beat AE-Kissat-MAB); run only at phase exits (§9) and before any
# external claim.
#
# UNLIKE Tiers B and C this list is NOT screened or selected: it is the complete
# published tracks, so no solver runs were involved in building it and no
# instance may be dropped for being slow, huge, or hard to proof-check.
#
# Runner settings come from tools/bench/run.py, not from this file:
#   timeout 5000 s (TIER_TIMEOUT["D"]), memory cap 16 GB (TIER_MEM_GB["D"]
#   => cpus-per-task = ceil(16/8) = 2 under the 8 GB/core rule, §8.1),
#   proof-checker budget max(3600, 4 x 5000) = 20000 s (§8.1 decision (d0bb6ec)).
# Fetch instances: tools/bench/fetch.py --tier D   (hashes in D.sources)
#
# SOURCE QUERY (Global Benchmark Database, https://benchmark-database.de):
#   curl https://benchmark-database.de/getdatabase  -> meta sqlite (2026-08-03)
#   hashes:   SELECT hash FROM track WHERE value IN ('main_2024','main_2025')
#   filename: JOIN the hash->value table `filename`
#   status:   features.result in (sat, unsat, unknown)
#   NB: use the `track` TABLE, never features.track — that column holds hashes,
#   not track names (same trap recorded in the Tier B expansion report).
#
# COLUMN 2 = EXPECTED STATUS.  `result = unknown` instances are RETAINED (71 of
# them), because the full track is the point of Tier D.  This is safe: run.py's
# docstring documents `<SAT|UNSAT|UNKNOWN>` as the accepted alphabet, and
# run_instance guards its correctness check with `if expected in ("SAT",
# "UNSAT")` — so an UNKNOWN instance is never flagged as a mismatch, is scored
# solved whenever we answer, and its UNSAT proofs are still checked/scored
# exactly like every other instance.  An UNKNOWN we solve is a real solve; it is
# also the one class where our answer has no independent oracle, so any UNKNOWN
# we close should be cross-checked (proof for UNSAT, model check for SAT) before
# being reported.
#
# COLUMN 3 = REFERENCE SECONDS, all 0.0 = NOT MEASURED.  Tiers B/C carry a
# kissat median there; a Tier D reference column would cost two full gate runs
# (~an afternoon each) and run.py never reads this field (it is parsed, stored,
# and only ever unpacked as `_`; PAR-2 uses measured time).  Fill it in from the
# first clean cluster kissat run if it ever becomes useful.
#
# DEDUPLICATION.  GBD is content-addressed and 7 instances appear in BOTH tracks
# (identical CNF re-used in SC25), so the union is 793 distinct instances, not
# 800.  They are listed ONCE, in the `shared` block below, and each track's own
# 400 is exactly reconstructible:
#     SC 2024 set (400) = shared block (7) + main_2024-only block (393)
#     SC 2025 set (400) = shared block (7) + main_2025-only block (393)   <- T4
# run.py has no per-track flag; recompute a per-track PAR-2 by filtering
# results.json on the block membership recorded here (and in D.sources).
#
# FILENAMES.  10 hashes carry several GBD filenames (the same CNF submitted
# under different names, e.g. goldcrest-and/or/xor-9 are one file); the
# lexicographically smallest name is used, deterministically.  All 793 names —
# and their decompressed stems, which run.py stages flat — are distinct.
#
# COMPOSITION (793): 345 SAT / 377 UNSAT / 71 UNKNOWN
#   SC 2024 set (400): 179 SAT / 208 UNSAT / 13 UNKNOWN
#   SC 2025 set (400): 172 SAT / 170 UNSAT / 58 UNKNOWN
#   118 distinct GBD families.
#
# OVERLAP WITH THE SMALLER TIERS (recorded so nobody reads a Tier D gate as
# independent confirmation of a Tier B/C result): 18 of Tier B's 28 and 19 of
# Tier C's 30 instances are also in Tier D.
#
# --- shared: in BOTH main_2024 and main_2025 (7) — counts toward each track's 400 ---
